  "evidence": "TEXT_G: 31% flat tax merging income tax and NI; workfare for some claimants; allow earnings up to the tax threshold without losing the Basic Cash Benefit; restrict benefits to citizens/residents of five years; retain/expand grammar and specialist schools with broader merit assessments; school/health/training vouchers; “life means life,” three strikes, end early release, double prison capacity; protect frontline NHS and restore free eye tests/dental checks; five‑year settlement freeze, long‑term immigration cap with time‑limited permits; 40% defence spending increase and maintain nuclear deterrent. TEXT_C: Reintroduce corporal and capital punishment; halt immigration from Muslim‑majority countries; repeal race/equalities legislation and restrict multilingual official documents; end foreign aid; daily school assemblies on traditional values; ban non‑stunned ritual slaughter; nationalise telecoms and reverse rail privatisation; selective tariffs and levies on large supermarkets; protect NHS and raise pensions to £150/week tied to earnings.",
  "reasoning": "Because the electorate’s strongest weight is proportionality followed by care and then authority, TEXT_G’s emphasis on merit and contribution (flat tax, workfare, grammar schools), in‑group care (protecting NHS/pensions), and firm but conventional law‑and‑order aligns more closely with that profile. TEXT_C prioritises authority and sanctity and hard group‑boundary policies (corporal/capital punishment, bans on Muslim immigration, repeal of equalities law) and leans toward statist economics, which pulls it away from the profile’s top emphasis on proportionality and balanced care.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "winner": "TEXT_G"
}
